\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The compact real Spin projection is a two-sheeted cover}

\p{For #{n\geq0}, let

##{Q_n^+(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2}

on #{\mathbb R^n}, and give its actual vector-generated group
#{\Spin(Q_n^+)} the subspace topology from the finite-dimensional real
Clifford algebra. This group is compact for every #{n}; it is
path-connected for #{n\geq2}. For #{n\geq1}, the vector action is a
two-sheeted covering

##{\Spin(Q_n^+)\longrightarrow SO(n)}

with kernel #{\{1,-1\}}.}

\p{Here is the compactness mechanism. Cartan--Dieudonne writes every
special orthogonal transformation as a bounded even word of reflections.
Positive definiteness normalizes every reflecting vector to the unit
sphere. An additional unit-vector pair corrects the possible scalar
#{-1}, and pairs #{(e,e)} pad the lift to a fixed length. Clifford
multiplication therefore gives a continuous surjection

##{\bigl(S^{n-1}\times S^{n-1}\bigr)^{n+1}
\longrightarrow\Spin(Q_n^+)\qquad(n\geq1).}

For #{n=0}, the actual vector-generated carrier is the singleton group,
hence compact. For #{n\geq1}, the displayed source is compact, proving
compactness. For #{n\geq2} it is also
path-connected, proving path-connectedness of the target. The plane curve
in \ref{fcap-0038} independently joins #{1} to #{-1}.}

\p{The vector action is a continuous surjection onto the Hausdorff group
#{SO(n)}. Compactness makes it a quotient map. Its two-element kernel is
discrete and acts freely, so the quotient-covering theorem makes the
action a two-sheeted covering. The finite kernel alone would not prove
either the quotient-map or covering assertion.}

\p{\citet{Section 1.8, pp. 38--40}{gallier2014clifford} gives the compact
double cover for #{n\geq1} and path-connectedness for #{n\geq2}, using
generators with negative square in #{\operatorname{Cl}_{0,n}}.
\citet{I.2 and I.4, pp. 14, 19, 25}{lawson2016spin} use the same compact
sign through #{v^2=-q(v)}.}

\p{To compare their carrier with the positive-square model above, let
#{e_i^2=1} and
#{f_i^2=-1}. The complex map #{e_i\mapsto i f_i} restricts on real even
PBW words to

##{F(e_{i_1}\cdots e_{i_{2k}})
=(-1)^k f_{i_1}\cdots f_{i_{2k}}.}

The same formula is its inverse. It is a real linear homeomorphism,
preserves multiplication and reversal, and carries an even product of
real vectors to the corresponding even product up to a scalar #{-1}.
That scalar belongs to both actual carriers, since
#{e(-e)=-1} and #{ff=-1}. Thus #{F} and its inverse identify the actual
Spin carriers. Complexifying the vector action and cancelling the common
factor #{i} shows that they also intertwine the actions on
#{\mathbb R^n}. Gallier defines Spin through a normalizer condition; the
carrier-completion argument in \ref{fcap-0037}, applied
after this sign transfer, identifies that normalizer-defined group with
the selected vector-generated carrier. This translates the source
convention without identifying the two full real Clifford algebras.}

\p{TauCeti proves compactness for every #{n}, path-connectedness in
dimensions #{n+2}, and the quotient-covering and covering statements for
nonzero dimension:
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.instCompactSpaceRealCliffordSpinGroupZero},
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.pathConnectedSpace_realCliffordSpinGroupZero_add_two},
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isQuotientCoveringMap_realCliffordSpinDoubleCoverZero_rightHom},
and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isCoveringMap_realCliffordSpinDoubleCoverZero_rightHom}.
These exact results concern the positive form and the finite-dimensional
carrier topology; they do not assert simple connectedness.}
